WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.840
Clinical trials are studies by&nbsp;
scientists that test new ways to prevent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.880
detect, diagnose, or treat disease.&nbsp;
Scientists ask volunteers to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.440
test new medicines or ways of&nbsp;
doing things during these trials.&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.080
Some of the things that&nbsp;
clinical trials test include:&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:26.080
New devices, such as blood glucose testers for&nbsp;
people with diabetes, pacemakers, or implants.&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.240
New drugs, such as painkillers,&nbsp;
medicines that lower your blood pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.200
or treatments for cancer.
New vaccines,&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.440
new ways to do surgery or other procedures,&nbsp;
such as a new kind of knee replacement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.200
or surgery to repair the heart&nbsp;
after someone has a heart attack and&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.320
new routines to improve a person’s health,&nbsp;
such as changes to diet and exercise

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.960
Clinical trials have helped us learn&nbsp;
how to live longer with illnesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.560
like cancer, high blood pressure, or&nbsp;
diabetes. Clinical trials have also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.440
helped us use vaccines to eliminate some&nbsp;
diseases like polio in the United States.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.720
Each clinical trial has a research plan that&nbsp;
explains in detail how the study will be run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.040
This plan includes information about who&nbsp;
the scientist will ask to participate.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.080
Sometimes scientists are looking for people who&nbsp;
have a specific disease to participate in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.680
clinical trial. Sometimes scientists need healthy&nbsp;
people to participate in the clinical trial.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.440
The purpose of a clinical trial&nbsp;
is to determine if the treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.240
prevention, or behavior changes that are&nbsp;
being studied are safe and effective.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.600
Clinical trials are reviewed, approved,&nbsp;
and monitored by a group of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.080
not involved in the study. This group makes&nbsp;
sure that the study is being conducted in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.520
ethical manner and that the rights and&nbsp;
welfare of the participants are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.320
protected. These groups are called&nbsp;
Institutional Review Boards or IRBs.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.600
In the United States, a clinical trial&nbsp;
must have an IRB if it is studying a drug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.040
biological product, or medical device&nbsp;
that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:07.840
(FDA) regulates, or if the clinical trial is&nbsp;
funded or conducted by the federal government.

